    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-2719",
      "problem": "Order the sides of the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ above from the shortest to the longest.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14163584/assets/2ad883fbaaa6fe0ffa87cbfc4b070f6e72ec3b4d62286ede37e3380f8fd6df25.png)",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the missing angle $\\angle A.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+{80}^{∘}+{75}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={180}^{∘}-{155}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={25}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m\\angle A <      m\\angle C <      m\\angle B.$\n\nSince $\\angle B$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overline{CA}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSince $\\angle A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overline{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC < BA < CA\n$$\n\nand therefore the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overline{BC}, \\overline{BA}, \\overline{CA}.\n$$",
